{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 5,
  "simulation_id": "sim-05",
  "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B025",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B014",
    "B028"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration has changed from B014 to B025. The fluctuation theorems remain an exceptionally elegant set of universal nonequilibrium relations, but the exchange makes the contrast in evidential closure important: optical lattice clocks unite a sharply stated physical mechanism, defining experimental realization, and durable independent reproducibility. A citation restricted to lattice clocks recognizes a demonstrated capability rather than a prospective redefinition of the second or future applications. Katori's magic-wavelength concept and Ye's defining experimental development provide a compact attribution that is presently more defensible than the exact priority allocation among the successive fluctuation-theorem formulations.",
    "case_against": "B025 must still be delimited carefully from the longer history of optical frequency metrology, and historical review should test whether early implementation contributions make the two-name attribution incomplete. The achievement is principally an instrumental and metrological breakthrough, not a new universal law; B014 therefore remains the stronger alternative if documentary work confirms that Evans, Jarzynski, and Crooks alone capture the indispensable theoretical chain.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that demonstrated cross-laboratory reproducibility and fundamental measurement capability establish the maturity of optical lattice clocks without waiting for formal redefinition of the second.",
        "response": "I agree with this maturity criterion. In statistical physics, a mechanism becomes compelling when it survives changes of realization; similarly, the significance here lies in reproducible control of systematic shifts and of many atoms, not in an eventual administrative metrology decision. This directly resolves my former concern that the clocks might be rewarded mainly for anticipated consequences."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson emphasizes the complementary attribution of Katori's magic-wavelength concept and Ye's experimental development, while cautioning that the achievement must be bounded against broader optical-frequency standards.",
        "response": "This is the decisive attribution comparison with B014. The proposed clock citation can be narrowly tied to the lattice-clock concept and its defining realization, whereas the fluctuation-theorem case requires more difficult adjudication among related but successive exact relations. I retain Johansson's boundary caution: a historical review must establish that the narrowed B025 wording does not exclude an equally indispensable early contributor."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson regards dynamical mean-field theory as quantitatively powerful for correlated materials but considers its distributed lineage and approximation-framework character a barrier to a secure three-person prize.",
        "response": "I continue to agree. DMFT has the kind of self-consistent reduction to an essential mechanism that I find scientifically powerful, and it remains my leading theoretical alternative after B014. But its intellectual lineage is less cleanly compressed into three names than either the two-person B025 proposal or, provisionally, the successive B014 formulations."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether archival historical review confirms that Katori and Ye alone provide a complete attribution for a citation limited specifically to optical lattice clocks.",
      "Whether the proposed wording sufficiently separates the lattice-clock breakthrough from the broader development of optical frequency standards.",
      "Whether priority evidence for Evans, Jarzynski, and Crooks would ultimately make B014 a more securely attributable theoretical achievement than it presently appears."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile. My University of Gothenburg environment is connected broadly to committee colleagues Göran Johansson and Eva Olsson, but no direct professional or institutional connection to the proposed B025 laureates is identified."
  }
}
